instructions.F5:
You are looking at a software repository in the current folder. A ticket was filed against it:

Title: {title}

{description}

Do not change any files and do not try to do the ticket. Find the source files, and the functions in them, that would have to change to do it. Use {tools} to look through the code.

When you are done, end your reply with one JSON block, and nothing after it:
```json
{"files": ["path/relative/to/the/repository", "..."], "locations": ["path:line", "..."], "answer": "one short paragraph saying what would change and why"}
```
List at most 8 files, the most important first.

grader.prompt:
You are grading an answer to a question about a codebase. You are shown the question, the code that answers it (the reference), and the answer to grade. You do not know who wrote the answer.

Question: {question}

Reference code ({file}, lines {start} to {end}):
```
{code}
```

Answer to grade:
{answer}

Is the answer correct about what the reference code does, in the way the question asks? Minor omissions are fine; a wrong claim about the behaviour is not. Reply with one JSON object and nothing else: {"correct": true|false, "why": "one sentence"}.
